    topic: "Festival programme",
    audioText: "Welcome to the programme announcement for this year's Silk and Spice Festival in Margilan, which opens on Friday and runs for four days. I will go through the main events, and I should start with a correction to the printed leaflet. It says that the opening ceremony begins at six o'clock, but it will in fact begin at seven, because the mayor has to attend a council meeting first. Please tell your friends.\n\nFriday evening begins with a parade from the old bazaar to the central square, led by drummers and dancers in traditional costume. Do not bring cars; the roads are closed from five. Then, at half past eight, the folk singer Zulfiya Rahimova will perform on the main stage. Tickets for this concert are free, but you must collect them in advance from the tourist office, and only two per person are allowed.\n\nOn Saturday, the festival becomes more practical. There are workshops all day: silk weaving at ten, pottery at noon, and cooking at three. The cooking class has been very popular, so it has moved from the small kitchen to the school hall, which holds sixty people instead of twenty. Each workshop costs fifteen thousand sums, except weaving, which is thirty thousand because of the materials. Children under twelve may join free if accompanied by an adult. In the evening there is a film screening in the park. The organisers originally planned an outdoor showing, but the forecast promises rain, so it will be inside the cultural centre, which means space is limited and you should arrive early.\n\nSunday is the day for the food market, where over eighty stalls will sell sweets, spices, bread and dried fruit. I personally recommend the stall near the fountain which sells honey from the Fergana hills; it was the best I have tasted. Vegetarians should know that the plov competition at noon is meat based, though there will be a vegetable version later, around two. The winner of the competition receives a golden spoon and a year of free flour from a local mill, which, to be honest, seems like a very practical prize.\n\nThe final day, Monday, is quieter, with a poetry reading in the morning and a closing concert at five. Many visitors leave early on Monday, which I think is a mistake, because the closing concert is often the highlight. Last year the organisers surprised everyone with a fireworks display that lasted twenty minutes.\n\nA few practical notes. Parking is available behind the railway station, with a free shuttle bus every fifteen minutes. Toilets and first aid points are marked on the map, and a lost property office will be open in the library. Please bring a reusable bottle; the organisers have installed free water points to reduce plastic waste, and single use bottles will not be sold. We look forward to seeing you.",
